"PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07182v1)\nciteturn26948view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view5\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 494\nL71: Process reward models provide step-level supervision for multi-step reasoning. Early work like PRM800K (cite43†Song et al., 2025 ) relied on costly human annotation. Automated approaches subsequently emerged to reduce this cost: Math-Shepherd (cite44†Wang et al., 2024 ) uses self-consistency sampling and symbolic verification, while Omega PRM (cite45†Luo et al., 2024 ) leverages MCTS-guided search to explore reasoning trajectories.\nL72: Recent methods like Qwen2.5-Math-PRM (cite46†Zhang et al., 2025 ) employ LLM-based judgments to generate soft labels, and EDU-PRM (cite47†Cao et al., 2025 ) uses entropy as fork points in MCTS searches. These advances enable scalable process supervision despite potential label noise.\nL73: ## 3 PRPO\nL74: \nL75: cite48†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Comparison between GRPO and PRPO.\nL76: \nL77: To leverage process-level rewards from PRMs in critic-free policy optimization, PRPO combines semantic-based segmentation with a distribution alignment mechanism that fuses outcome and process advantages. The overall framework is shown in Figure cite49†2 .\nL78: ### 3.1 Semantic-Based Sequence Segmentation\nL79: \nL80: Process segmentation for PRM evaluation has often relied on explicit markers such as “Step” (cite46†Zhang et al., 2025 ). This heuristic generalizes poorly when a model is not trained to emit such markers at every reasoning stage. To obtain a more robust and model-agnostic segmentation, we use token-level entropy to split the output sequence into segments and evaluate each segment with a PRM.\nL81: Recent studies demonstrate that token-level entropy is effective for segmenting LLM outputs (cite40†Liu et al., 2025a ; cite47†Cao et al., 2025 ; cite50†Wang et al., 2025 ). cite50†Wang et al. (2025) find that entropy spikes frequently align with logical connectors (e.g., “wait”, “however”, “nevertheless”) that bridge deductive sentences. ETTRL (cite40†Liu et al., 2025a ) similarly uses entropy spikes as fork points in rollout search, achieving better performance than traditional rollout within the TTRL framework.\nL82: These results suggest that entropy peaks offer reliable semantic boundaries.\nL83: For LLM outputs, the entropy of each token is generally calculated as\nL84: \nL85:  | $$E_{k}=-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}p(x_{k,i})\\log(p(x_{k,i}))$$  |\nL86: \nL87: Leveraging this property, we pick the top $k$ spike points—each at least $m$ tokens apart—as split positions and evaluate the process-level reward for every resulting segment with a PRM.\nL88: Although it is straightforward to think entropy can decrease during training, which may make this segmentation method less reliable. However, based on empirical experiments done by (cite50†Wang et al., 2025 ), the tokens with higher entropy generally increase their entropy during training, while other tokens stay at a relatively stable entropy level. This means that the entropy spike points become more significant during training, thus justifying the reliability of this segmentation method.\nL89: The detailed step about how this semantic-based segmentation is implemented is in Appendix cite21†B .\nL90: ### 3.2 Aligning Outcome-level and Process-level Reward\nL91: \nL92: Consider a sampled trajectory $\\tau$ of length $T$ segmented into $M$ logical units $s_{i}=[t_{i-1},t_{i})$ with PRM rewards $r_{\\text{process}}^{i}$. Tokens within segment $s_{i}$ share the same PRM score. The segment-level process reward is expanded to tokens by setting, for any $t\\in s_{i}$,\nL93: \nL94:  | $$z_{\\text{process},t}(\\tau)=\\frac{r_{\\text{process}}^{i}-\\mu_{\\text{prior\\_process}}}{\\sigma_{\\text{prior\\_process}}},$$  |\nL95: giving the token vector $\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{process}}(\\tau)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T}$, where $\\mu_{\\text{prior\\_process}}$ and $\\sigma_{\\text{prior\\_process}}$ are the mean and standard deviation of the prior distribution of the PRM output. In practice, as the PRM we used (Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B) has its output value normalized within [0,1], we use the expected PRM output (0.5) as the mean and the standard deviation of a uniform distribution (0.289) for normalization.\nL96: The sparse outcome reward $R_{\\text{outcome}}(\\tau)$ is normalized using prior statistics to obtain the beta coefficient. As we need to fuse the outcome advantage with process advantage, we follow Dr. GRPO (cite51†Liu et al., 2025b ) and only use the mean value for normalization to avoid the scaling bias of the standard deviation which may cause problem in advantage aligning:\nL97: \nL98:  | $$\\beta(\\tau)=R_{\\text{outcome}}(\\tau)-\\mu_{\\text{rollout}}$$  |\nL99: where $\\mu_{\\text{rollout}}$ is the mean value of the outcome reward in each rollout group, which is defined as:\nL100: \nL101:  | $$\\mu_{\\text{rollout}}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}R_{\\text{outcome}}(\\tau^{(j)})$$  |\nL102: \nL103: and broadcast this scalar to every token position of $\\tau$.\nL104: #### Premature Collapse in Process-Only Reward\nL105: \nL106: Let $\\pi_{\\theta}$ be an autoregressive policy and $x_{0:T}$ a sampled sequence. Its log-likelihood decomposes as\nL107: \nL108:  | $$\\log p_{\\theta}(x_{0:T})=\\sum_{t=0}^{T}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t}).$$  |\nL109: \nL110: The process reward model outputs process rewards $r_{t}$ with standardized advantage $A_{t}:=r_{t}-\\mu_{\\text{process}}$. Under process-only optimization,\nL111:  | $\\displaystyle J_{\\text{proc}}(\\theta)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\Big[\\sum_{t=0}^{T}A_{t}\\,\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})\\Big],$  |  | (1)\nL112:  | $\\displaystyle\\nabla_{\\theta}J_{\\text{proc}}(\\theta)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\Big[\\sum_{t=0}^{T}A_{t}\\,\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})\\Big].$  |  | (2)\nL113: Based on our observation of the process-level and outcome-level advantage during the RL training, we proposed the following hypothesis that usually causes the collapse of model trained with RL algorithm using only process-level signal:\nL114: ###### Hypothesis 1 (Empirical Premature Collapse Condition during RL training with Process-Only Reward).\nL115: \nL116: For a generative sequence with token-level advantages $\\{A_{t}\\}_{t=0}^{T}$ where $A_{t}=r_{t}-\\mu_{\\text{process}}$ (process reward $r_{t}$ relative to mean $\\mu_{\\text{process}}$), if there exists a position $t^{\\star}\\in[1,T]$ such that:\nL117: \nL118:   1. 1.\nL119: The parameters $a$ and $b$ are sampled from the same sequence: $a=\\left|\\frac{1}{t^{\\star}}\\sum_{i=0}^{t^{\\star}-1}A_{i}\\right|$ (absolute mean advantage over prefix $[0,t^{\\star})$) and $b=A_{t^{\\star}}$ (advantage at position $t^{\\star}$), where $a$ reflects early tokens with process rewards below the mean (negative advantage) and $b$ reflects a token with process reward above the mean (positive advantage).\nL120: \nL121:   2. 2.\nL122: Early tokens have negative average advantage: $\\frac{1}{t^{\\star}}\\sum_{i=0}^{t^{\\star}-1}A_{i}=-a<0$ with $a>0$, indicating process rewards below the mean.\nL123: \nL124:   3. 3.\nL125: \nL126: Token at $t^{\\star}$ has positive advantage: $A_{t^{\\star}}=b>0$, indicating process reward above the mean.\nL127: \nL128:   4. 4.\nL129: \nL130: The cumulative negative magnitude exceeds the positive benefit: $a\\cdot t^{\\star}>b$.\nL131: Then the conflicting gradient signals from early negative advantages ($-a$) and later positive advantage ($b$) within the same sequence cause $p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})$ to decrease, leading to premature collapse.\nL132: \nL133: The mathematical reason for this hypothesis is shown in Appendix cite20†A L134: Interpretation Negative advantages hit every prefix term multiplicatively, while the single positive advantage at $t^{\\star}$ cannot compensate for the accumulated decay. Heavy-tailed or outlier negative rewards (large $a$) accelerate the collapse.\nL135: Based on this hypothesis, it can be witnessed that the root problem of the collapse of model trained with process-only reward is the fluctuation of process rewards, which can cause conflict of advantage signal.\nL136: PURE (cite38†Cheng et al., 2025 ) resolves this problem by simply choosing the min value of the process reward model output, which surely can resolve possible collapse of process reward only training, but will lose much information of the different process rewards along the whole output sequence, especially for the higher ones which are usually abandoned.\nL137: Practical observation We actually did an experiment on Qwen2.5-Math-7B model trained with process advantages alone, and found that it empirically shortened responses and eventually produced meaningless short strings, matching the hypothesis.\nL138: Implication Even perfectly estimated process rewards are unsafe when used alone with relative advantages. Necessary but relatively poor processes get suppressed, and locally good-but-misaligned processes get over-weighted. A prior or alignment term (next section) is needed to prevent collapse. The prior keeps high-prior processes from being suppressed, while distribution alignment ties process rewards to outcome rewards so the final outcome advantage safely guides process-level updates.\nL139: #### Distribution Alignment\nL140: \nL141: PRPO shifts the process-advantage distribution using the outcome advantage, as shown in the bottom of Figure cite49†2 :\nL142: \nL143:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\mathcal{P}_{\\text{process}}^{\\prime}]=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathcal{P}_{\\text{process}}]+\\mathbb{E}[\\mathcal{P}_{\\text{outcome}}],$$  |\nL144: \nL145: producing a fused per-token advantage\nL146: \nL147:  | $$\\mathbf{AF}(\\tau)=\\mathbf{z}_{\\text{process}}(\\tau)+\\beta(\\tau),$$  |\nL148: Concretely, $AF_{t}(\\tau)=z_{\\text{process},t}(\\tau)+\\beta(\\tau)$ for every token position $t$, so each token inherits the segment-normalized PRM score plus a broadcasted, group-centered outcome shift. The policy loss becomes\nL149: \nL150:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{PRPO}}=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau_{i}}\\left[\\mathbf{AF}(\\tau)(x_{t}^{(i)})\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}^{(i)}|x_{<t}^{(i)})}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(x_{t}^{(i)}|x_{<t}^{(i)})}\\right].$$  |\nL151: ## 4 Experiments\nL152: \nL153: Table 1: Time cost analysis when one training use the PRM server and three training use the server simultaneously.\nL154: Method  | Time Cost (s)\nL155: --- | ---\nL156: w/o PRM step time  | 46.8\nL157: w PRM step time (1 training)  | 80.8\nL158: w PRM step time (3 trainings)  | 84.0\nL159: \nL160: (a) GRPO vs GRPO+PRPO.\nL161: \nL162: (b) PRM-Avg vs PRM-Avg+PRPO.\nL163: Figure 3: Training accuracy on MATH. PRPO steadily improves both baselines. Table 2: Accuracy comparison across datasets (%).\nL164: Model  | Algorithm  | MATH pass@1  | AMC2023 mean32  | AIME2025 mean32  | AIME2024 mean32\nL165: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: Qwen-M-1.5  | 4-shot (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 )  | 49.80  | -  | -  | -\nL167: GRPO  | 61.20  | 47.89  | 7.00  | 7.60\nL168: PRM-Avg  | 64.0  | 46.33  | 6.25  | 9.69\nL169: PURE  | 64.2  | 48.44  | 5.39  | 11.56\nL170: GRPO+PRPO  | 64.4  | 50.78  | 4.20  | 12.71\nL171: PRM-Avg+PRPO  | 66.00  | 51.8  | 6.03  | 11.46\nL172: Qwen-M-7  | 4-shot (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 )  | 55.40  | -  | -  | -\nL173: GRPO  | 69.80  | 57.82  | 7.65  | 17.81\nL174: PRM-Avg  | 71.00  | 55.8  | 8.94  | 9.69\nL175: PURE  | 71.4  | 55.62  | 9.81  | 18.85\nL176: GRPO+PRPO  | 72.00  | 56.72  | 10.99  | 23.65\nL177: PRM-Avg+PRPO  | 70.60  | 59.38  | 11.21  | 22.08\nL178: Table 3: Pass rate comparison on Qwen2.5-Math-7B.\nL179: Method  | Pass Rate (%)  | Average\nL180: --- | --- | ---\nL181: pass@8  | pass@16  | pass@32  | pass@64  | pass@128\nL182: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL183: AIME 2024\nL184: GRPO  | 36.67  | 50.00  | 60.00  | 56.67  | 63.33  | $53.33$\nL185: PRMAVG  | 26.67  | 33.33  | 33.33  | 43.33  | 53.33  | $38.00$\nL186: PURE  | 50.00  | 50.00  | 63.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | $57.33$\nL187: GRPO+PRPO  | 46.67  | 53.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | 60.00  | $56.67$\nL188: PRMAVG+PRPO  | 46.67  | 53.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | 70.00  | 58.67\nL189: AIME 2025\nL190: GRPO  | 24.14  | 27.59  | 37.93  | 27.59  | 41.38  | $31.73$\nL191: PRMAVG  | 20.69  | 24.14  | 27.59  | 34.48  | 37.93  | $28.97$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07182v1)\nciteturn26948view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view5\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 494\nL111:  | $\\displaystyle J_{\\text{proc}}(\\theta)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\Big[\\sum_{t=0}^{T}A_{t}\\,\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})\\Big],$  |  | (1)\nL112:  | $\\displaystyle\\nabla_{\\theta}J_{\\text{proc}}(\\theta)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\Big[\\sum_{t=0}^{T}A_{t}\\,\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})\\Big].$  |  | (2)\nL113: Based on our observation of the process-level and outcome-level advantage during the RL training, we proposed the following hypothesis that usually causes the collapse of model trained with RL algorithm using only process-level signal:\nL114: ###### Hypothesis 1 (Empirical Premature Collapse Condition during RL training with Process-Only Reward).\nL115: \nL116: For a generative sequence with token-level advantages $\\{A_{t}\\}_{t=0}^{T}$ where $A_{t}=r_{t}-\\mu_{\\text{process}}$ (process reward $r_{t}$ relative to mean $\\mu_{\\text{process}}$), if there exists a position $t^{\\star}\\in[1,T]$ such that:\nL117: \nL118:   1. 1.\nL119: The parameters $a$ and $b$ are sampled from the same sequence: $a=\\left|\\frac{1}{t^{\\star}}\\sum_{i=0}^{t^{\\star}-1}A_{i}\\right|$ (absolute mean advantage over prefix $[0,t^{\\star})$) and $b=A_{t^{\\star}}$ (advantage at position $t^{\\star}$), where $a$ reflects early tokens with process rewards below the mean (negative advantage) and $b$ reflects a token with process reward above the mean (positive advantage).\nL120: \nL121:   2. 2.\nL122: Early tokens have negative average advantage: $\\frac{1}{t^{\\star}}\\sum_{i=0}^{t^{\\star}-1}A_{i}=-a<0$ with $a>0$, indicating process rewards below the mean.\nL123: \nL124:   3. 3.\nL125: \nL126: Token at $t^{\\star}$ has positive advantage: $A_{t^{\\star}}=b>0$, indicating process reward above the mean.\nL127: \nL128:   4. 4.\nL129: \nL130: The cumulative negative magnitude exceeds the positive benefit: $a\\cdot t^{\\star}>b$.\nL131: Then the conflicting gradient signals from early negative advantages ($-a$) and later positive advantage ($b$) within the same sequence cause $p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})$ to decrease, leading to premature collapse.\nL132: \nL133: The mathematical reason for this hypothesis is shown in Appendix cite20†A L134: Interpretation Negative advantages hit every prefix term multiplicatively, while the single positive advantage at $t^{\\star}$ cannot compensate for the accumulated decay. Heavy-tailed or outlier negative rewards (large $a$) accelerate the collapse.\nL135: Based on this hypothesis, it can be witnessed that the root problem of the collapse of model trained with process-only reward is the fluctuation of process rewards, which can cause conflict of advantage signal.\nL136: PURE (cite38†Cheng et al., 2025 ) resolves this problem by simply choosing the min value of the process reward model output, which surely can resolve possible collapse of process reward only training, but will lose much information of the different process rewards along the whole output sequence, especially for the higher ones