)  | 80.8\nL158: w PRM step time (3 trainings)  | 84.0\nL159: \nL160: (a) GRPO vs GRPO+PRPO.\nL161: \nL162: (b) PRM-Avg vs PRM-Avg+PRPO.\nL163: Figure 3: Training accuracy on MATH. PRPO steadily improves both baselines. Table 2: Accuracy comparison across datasets (%).\nL164: Model  | Algorithm  | MATH pass@1  | AMC2023 mean32  | AIME2025 mean32  | AIME2024 mean32\nL165: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: Qwen-M-1.5  | 4-shot (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 )  | 49.80  | -  | -  | -\nL167: GRPO  | 61.20  | 47.89  | 7.00  | 7.60\nL168: PRM-Avg  | 64.0  | 46.33  | 6.25  | 9.69\nL169: PURE  | 64.2  | 48.44  | 5.39  | 11.56\nL170: GRPO+PRPO  | 64.4  | 50.78  | 4.20  | 12.71\nL171: PRM-Avg+PRPO  | 66.00  | 51.8  | 6.03  | 11.46\nL172: Qwen-M-7  | 4-shot (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 )  | 55.40  | -  | -  | -\nL173: GRPO  | 69.80  | 57.82  | 7.65  | 17.81\nL174: PRM-Avg  | 71.00  | 55.8  | 8.94  | 9.69\nL175: PURE  | 71.4  | 55.62  | 9.81  | 18.85\nL176: GRPO+PRPO  | 72.00  | 56.72  | 10.99  | 23.65\nL177: PRM-Avg+PRPO  | 70.60  | 59.38  | 11.21  | 22.08\nL178: Table 3: Pass rate comparison on Qwen2.5-Math-7B.\nL179: Method  | Pass Rate (%)  | Average\nL180: --- | --- | ---\nL181: pass@8  | pass@16  | pass@32  | pass@64  | pass@128\nL182: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL183: AIME 2024\nL184: GRPO  | 36.67  | 50.00  | 60.00  | 56.67  | 63.33  | $53.33$\nL185: PRMAVG  | 26.67  | 33.33  | 33.33  | 43.33  | 53.33  | $38.00$\nL186: PURE  | 50.00  | 50.00  | 63.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | $57.33$\nL187: GRPO+PRPO  | 46.67  | 53.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | 60.00  | $56.67$\nL188: PRMAVG+PRPO  | 46.67  | 53.33  | 60.00  | 63.33  | 70.00  | 58.67\nL189: AIME 2025\nL190: GRPO  | 24.14  | 27.59  | 37.93  | 27.59  | 41.38  | $31.73$\nL191: PRMAVG  | 20.69  | 24.14  | 27.59  | 34.48  | 37.93  | $28.97$\nL192: PURE  | 27.5  | 31.03  | 34.48  | 37.93  | 44.83  | $35.15$\nL193: GRPO+PRPO  | 31.03  | 31.03  | 31.03  | 37.93  | 48.28  | 35.86\nL194: PRMAVG+PRPO  | 20.69  | 34.48  | 34.48  | 41.38  | 41.38  | $34.48$\nL195: AMC 2023\nL196: GRPO  | 77.50  | 77.50  | 82.50  | 90.00  | 92.50  | $84.00$\nL197: PRMAVG  | 80.00  | 80.00  | 85.00  | 87.50  | 87.50  | $84.00$\nL198: PURE  | 90.00  | 87.50  | 90.00  | 95.00  | 97.50  | $92.00$\nL199: GRPO+PRPO  | 85.00  | 85.00  | 90.00  | 97.50  | 97.50  | $91.00$\nL200: PRMAVG+PRPO  | 85.00  | 90.00  | 95.00  | 97.50  | 100.00  | 93.50\nL201: Table 4: Ablation study (%).\nL202: Model  | Algorithm  | MATH pass@1\nL203: --- | --- | ---\nL204: Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B  | GRPO+PRPO w Random Split  | 2.4\nL205: GRPO+PRPO w Uniform Split  | 29.8\nL206: GRPO+PRPO w Entropy Based split  | 64.4\nL207: Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B  | GRPO+PRPO w Relative distribution for process reward  | 64.0\nL208: GRPO+PRPO w Predefined distribution for process reward  | 64.4\nL209: We train on 8 H200 GPUs with batch size 128, learning rate $1\\times 10^{-6}$, KL loss coefficient 0.001, clip ratio 0.2, maximum new tokens 2048, and rollout number 8. For segmentation, we pick five entropy spikes that are at least ten tokens apart ($k{=}5,n{=}10$).\nL210: For PRM, we choose Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B as the PRM model, and deploy it on 8 H200 GPUs according to official guidance to normalize the output value with $[0,1]$ range. We did an analysis on the time cost of PRM when training Qwen-Math-1.5B in Table cite53†1 . In this experiment, we take GRPO as model training without PRM, and PRPO+GRPO as model training with PRM. We trained each model for 1 epoch and calculated the average time cost for each step.\nL211: Even with PRM and training three models simultaneously, the additional time cost is about 37 seconds each step (46.8 s vs 84.0 s). Comparing to the benefit and higher upper bound brought by PRM, the time cost increase is acceptable.\nL212: We compare PRPO with GRPO and PRM-Avg (cite36†Zou et al., 2025 ; cite37†Ren, 2025 ). PRM-Avg adds the average process reward to the outcome reward, which is a common practice in recent work of using PRM in RL training like ReasonFlux (cite36†Zou et al., 2025 ) and lsrl (cite37†Ren, 2025 ). It’s worth noting that there’s a slight difference between lsrl and reasonflux, where lsrl use a discounted sum of process reward, while reasonflux use naive average of process reward.\nL213: As reasonflux is a more recent and widely referenced work, we use naive average used in reasonflux for PRM-avg. As PRPO provides another insight of advantage calculation, it can apply to both baselines. We also compare our algorithm with the current state-of-the-art baseline PURE (cite38†Cheng et al., 2025 ) algorithm, which is the only critic-free algorithm that tried to realize process-level credit assignment in critic-free framework.\nL214: We implement PURE with the exact same reward and advantage settings in the original work to reach the optimal performance, where the output reward signal is 0-1 reward, and the assignment temperature is set as 0.1. We evaluate performance of each algorithm on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B and Qwen2.5-Math-7B (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 ).\nL215: We conduct our algorithm and train the models with the VeRL training framework (cite54†Sheng et al., 2025 ).\nL216: Models are trained on the training split of MATH (cite55†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ) with 12000 samples with early stopping.\nL217: Evaluation covers MATH, AMC 2023, AIME 2025, and AIME 2024 (cite56†Art of Problem Solving, 2025a ; cite57†Art of Problem Solving, 2025b ) using greedy decoding (pass@1) for MATH as there is sufficient number of test cases in its test split (500 samples) and sampling 32 rollouts for AMC/AIME with temperature 0.7, top-p 0.9, and seeds fixed to 42 as there are limited test cases in AIME (30 samples each dataset) and AMC 2023 (40 samples).\nL218: For algorithms other than PURE, rule based outcome rewards are $\\pm 1$ based on SymPy-verified correctness, plus a length penalty beyond 1024 tokens. Process rewards come from Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B.\nL219: We draw the accuracy curve of different algorithms trained on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B in Figure cite58†3 . As shown in the figure, PRPO steadily improves both baselines, and generally fit quicker and reach higher accuracy than all baselines.\nL220: \nL221: The length penalty is described as follows:\nL222: \nL223:  | $$\\text{length\\_penalty}=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }\\text{length}\\leq 1024\\\\\nL224: \\frac{\\text{length}}{1024}&\\text{if }\\text{length}>1024\\end{cases}$$  |\nL225: As shown in Table cite59†2 , our PRPO algorithm consistently outperforms both GRPO and PRM-Avg baselines across all evaluated datasets and model sizes. Qwen-M-1.5 refers to Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B model, and Qwen-M-7 refers to Qwen2.5-Math-7B model. For Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B, PRPO improves MATH pass@1 accuracy from 61.20% (GRPO) to 64.4% (+3.2%), and further to 66.00% when combined with PRM-Avg (64.00% vs 66.00%).\nL226: On AMC2023 and AIME2024, our method also yields substantial gains, demonstrating its effectiveness in both standard and challenging mathematical benchmarks. Although our method does not perform as well as PRM-Avg on AIME2025, the gap is not significant (5.9% vs 7.00%), which can be attributed to capacity and fitting limits under dense supervision.\n"
